 you  can see that orange  circle over alaska and the pacific  northwest  that shows drying and warming during el  nino  we are in the middle of a la nina which  is the direct  opposite of el nino during el ninos you  also have  really dry uh weather over indonesia and  the rainforest air and you off  often see very large fires but this is  all coming from  the tropical pacific in that green area  so now we're going to look at  what sea level tells us about what's  going on with the tropical pacific with  the next slide  can you give me the next slide please  there we go so this is sea level  from 2015 to 2016 and you see  this this red which indicates high sea  level  now what does that mean what that means  is that there  is a rise a local rise in sea level and  in fact  it's almost um up to a meter in some  places in the tropical pacific  now that doesn't sound like that much  but it represents a huge amount of heat  in the ocean  if we think about water water can hold a  huge amount of heat and in fact when i  go to bed at night  and my feet are cold i use a hot water  bottle not a hot air bottle  so that to that about a meter or so a  little bit less than a meter of sea  level rise  actually represents warming of the ocean  from the surface down to  several hundred meters several hundred  feet or a hundred meters  that's a huge amount of warm water and  with that warm water  we can tickle that atmosphere or even  push it a little bit  and then see those impacts far from the  tropical pacific  so sea level is really important for  understanding what controls el nino and  la nina and the transitions  and can potentially help help us predict  it  so we're going to go ahead and start  talking about ocean currents  with the next animation so the  other thing about sea level that deirdre  mentioned is that it actually gives us a  really accurate  representative of this representation of  the surface currents in the ocean  so here's an animation of cir surface  currents and you can see  the um gulf of mexico where you see  this big eddy going around and that's  where deirdre was talking about  the impacts of sea level rise on  hurricanes  and then you see the gulf stream coming  up the eastern seaboard of the u.s  and going out into the north pacific i  mean the north atlantic  the really interesting thing about sea  level is that the sea level doesn't ride  downhill i mean the currents don't ride  downhill  they ride along the hill and the steeper  the hill  of sea level the faster the currents so  that animation ended up in my favorite  current the gulf stream which is key to  my research right now  so let's go on to the next slide and  start talking about the ocean's role in  climate and how  it exchanges heat with the atmosphere  now the global warming signal of from  excess carbon dioxide in the atmosphere  gives us about an excess of two watts  per meter squared over the ocean  now if you look over the gulf stream  once again east of the us  you might be able to see that um the  amount of heat that's leaving the ocean  and going to the  atmosphere is two orders of a magnitude  bigger  that's 200 watts per meter squared and  that also represents  uh area of the ocean that has large  variations in sea level  or the heat content of the upper ocean  and with that information of how that  heat content is changing  by observing sea level we can also learn  things about  how we can use this huge heat capacity  of the ocean to actually  understand what controls weather  over europe on time scales from  a month to several years  so sea level these sea level  measurements are not just about what's  going on  around the coast but they also tell us  about  what controls the climate over months to  years  and in particular what the role is for  controlling this climate variability and  change okay i'll send it back to you  marina  thank you so much luanne we are now  ready to take media questions  remember to press star one to get put 