ke noaa is doing but also for example  the copernicus marine environmental  monitoring service  cements will be doing ecmwf  the european center for a medium range  weather forecast will be  so settle six will start to be  incorporated in a lot of the user  products that are  created by other agencies as well  and i think that's what makes this  product so amazing is that everybody has  access to it across the globe which is  really incredible we've got our next  question from twitter  from ave valencia and the question is  why is its twin satellite being launched  five years apart would you like to take  that josh  uh yeah thanks for the question um we  need to get  uh as long a record as we can of sea  level we've been measuring sea level  since the early 90s  with these missions and each one has  just barely launched in time before the  next one  has aged and stopped working so  what's amazing about this satellite is  really two things first is that we're  launching it while  our previous satellite jason-3 is still  healthy  and young and then the second is that  we'll have another satellite in five  years  as sentinel 6 michael freilix starts to  age we'll be able to launch  sentinel 6b to take its place and extend  the record a full decade  and as i said before every decade is  like a whole new rate of global sea  level rise that we have to measure  all over again sea level rise isn't  going to go away anytime soon so we have  to keep measuring it  and the longer the record the better  thank you josh next from instagram  cedric asks how long will sentinel 6  operate for karen  as well as its uh its follow-on sentinel  6b  we anticipate we'll live uh about seven  years  so that's how we get to uh by launching  them five years apart that's how we get  to the decade-long  record that that josh just mentioned  great thank you karen and now denzel  on periscope asks what part of the ocean  is covered by this new satellite that  wasn't  before craig would you like to take that  question  it's a really good question the part of  the ocean that has not been covered  before  is the high resolution part of the ocean  which we can get at  by using our synthetic aperture radar  technologies  and this means that we can get down to  just um 300 meters in the along track  direction  and in certain parts of the ocean for  example um  would be in the eastern basins of um  in the southern pacific how a long wave  swell  rolls in from the antarctic uh and the  southern ocean currents  um and we can see that in the in the sar  data  and that hasn't been visible before in  the low resolution measurements  we've had in the past the orbit stays  exactly the same  as the predecessor mission is because as  josh manson and carol mentioned  we need to keep uh our spacecraft in  that orbit  to maintain the same measurements for  the long duration that's how we get the  precision of our sea level measurements  back to you  thank you craig now astro girl on  periscope  asks how much does sea water rise  each and every year luanne would you  like to take that  a few millimeters a year so on  accumulated over  the record we have about nine um  over the last century we have about nine  centimeters of sea level rise  and it's just that sea level rise is  going to continue to accelerate over  time  yes thank you luanne at moon to mars  quest  on twitter asks 2020 has produced a  robust hurricane season in the atlantic  basin  will future active hurricane activity  like  this year's affect the general average  sea height  when compared to the baseline deidre  would you like to take that  um i don't see the  hurricanes affecting the sea level it's  more that a higher sea level  is indicative of more heat that's  available to feed into the hurricanes  so it's the other way around if i got  the question right  yes thank you lakin on twitter  asks what is the deepest sea level  measured so far by the satellites that  have come  before sentinel 6.  josh would yo